                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F'(*) \simeq colim_D F
          %p
            Conversely, given a pullback diagram as above, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F' = f^* \circ G'
            (viewing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G'
            as a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \to C/Z
            and remembering that colimits in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C/Z
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+category#ColimitInSliceAreReflectedByColimitsInPlainCategory"} computed
            as colimits in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo ′
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha' : F' \Rightarrow G'
            the natural transformation induced by the pullback projections, which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equifibered+natural+transformation"} equifibered
            as a consequence of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+law+for+pullbacks"} pasting law for pullbacks
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^* \circ G'
            is a colimiting cocone, which is to say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            preserves
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi colim
                      %mi D
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} colim_D G
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#toposes Toposes
          %p
            The stability of all colimits is one of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud%27s+axioms"} Giraud's axioms
            that characterize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} 1-categorical context
            and Grothendieck-Rezk-Lurie
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical context
          %ul
            %li
              First observe that colimits are stable in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C =
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %li
              Now colimits are stable for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C =
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
              category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [S^{op},Set]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              are computed objectwise in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits+and+colimits+by+example"} limits and colimits by example
            %li Finally, stability of colimits is preserved in reflective subcategories, since the reflector preserves both colimits and pullbacks.
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, theorem 6.1.0.6 (3) ii)
          %h3#nontoposes Non-toposes
          %p
            More generally, colimits are stable in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            all have right adjoints. Conversely, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is cocomplete with all stable colimits, and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
            applies to all its slice categories, then it is locally cartesian closed.
          %p
            Colimits are also stable in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact
            infinitary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive category
          %p
            But colimits are not stable in, for instance,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C =
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
          %h2#relation_to_commutativity_and_distributivity Relation to commutativity and distributivity
          %p
            Although stability of colimits appears as a sort of “commutativity” between colimits and pullbacks, it is not literally an instance of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutativity+of+limits+and+colimits"} commutativity of limits and colimits
            %em example
            of the latter if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            of the diagram constant on a single object (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            this is a mild condition, requiring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            to be a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+category"} connected category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            this becomes an ever stronger condition; for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} colimits in an (infinity,1)-category
            it means that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} infinity-groupoid
            generated by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+homotopy+type"} contractible homotopy type
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "infinity-limit#EveryInfinityGroupoidIsHomotopyColimitOfConstantFunctorOverItself"} this corollary
          %p
            It
            %em is
            generally true that
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:IsoForPullbackStabilityOfColimis"} (1)
            is an example of
            = succeed ";" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributivity+of+limits+over+colimits"} distributivity of limits over colimits
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "distributivity+of+limits+over+colimits#PullbackStableColimits"} there
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+colimit"} van Kampen colimit
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback-stability"} pullback-stability
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
              (Section 6.1.1)
